I'll design an abstract/mathematical setting: a table of mean phenotype values for three genotypes across two environments, with a question requiring the student to explain the phenotypic variance pattern (genotype-by-environment interaction vs. environmental effect only).

Design: Three genotypes (G1, G2, G3) of a clonal plant, mean height (cm) at two temperatures (15°C, 25°C). Values: G1: 20, 30; G2: 24, 34; G3: 28, 38. Every genotype increases by exactly 10 cm, so the environment shifts the phenotype equally across genotypes and the lines are parallel (no interaction). Genotype differences persist in both environments.

The key is "Temperature changes the phenotype by the same amount in each genotype, while genotype differences persist in both environments, so both genetic and environmental factors contribute without interaction". The distractors are:
- phenotype is determined solely by genotype (the environment has no effect);
- the environment changes the DNA sequence;
- the environmental effect differs by genotype (an interaction is claimed, but the data are parallel).

